/* ==========================================================================
** CodeMirror 6 - emacs keymap
**
** The plugin's stylesheet is small on purpose: it only restyles the selection
** and the cursor, because the emacs binding's visible difference from the
** default is the MARK - the region between where you set the mark and where
** point is now. That region is drawn with the ordinary selection colour.
**
** So the selection wash is the whole surface here, and it matters more than it
** does in the default editor: with the mark set, the highlight can span the
** better part of a screen and stays there while you move around. It has to be
** faint enough to read a paragraph of code through and still obvious enough to
** tell you the mark is active. The accent at 22% is a little lighter than the
** ordinary selection for that reason.
** ========================================================================== */

.tc-editor-codemirror6 .cm-selectionBackground,
.tc-editor-codemirror6 .cm-selectionBackground * {
	background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--tpc-primary) 22%, transparent);
}

.tc-editor-codemirror6 .cm-cursor {
	border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--tpc-primary);
}

</$list>
